{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED on exact head bd81fbb15251ff949a8ebb984e70a8a933bcc729.\n\nFour prior functional findings are resolved. Disclosure now uses one native button primitive with aria-expanded and aria-controls; mouse open, nested reference navigation without toggling, second-click close, unreached plain text, and elided act detail are exercised. Replacing Disclosure's onClick with undefined makes the focused DOM test fail at ui/test/dom.test.mjs:619. Navigation now has one threadRoot rule that covers requests, promise/artifact first-basis ancestry, acts through their target, and unrooted/unknown records without inventing a request. Act sequence comes from the fold decision and the DOM test observes #7. List population survives an actual list unmount and return.\n\nThe availability blocker is not fully resolved:\n1. App builds a full RecordIndex, then Thread builds a second full RecordIndex for the same projection while that App index is still live. The condition says once per projection, but every thread view duplicates all statement, act, decision, artifact, review, reverse-provenance, and routing maps.\n2. buildRecordIndex constructs each reverse-provenance bucket with array spread on every dependent. A basis with k direct dependents copies 1+...+k entries, making the new supposedly bounded index O(k²). Get or create the bucket and push once.\n3. ElidedRecord still performs three full projection.find scans per displayed record, and actionsFor repeats commitment and decision scans per station, instead of using the index the head added.\n4. The reference limit is not load-bearing. In a detached exact-head worktree I changed REFERENCE_LIMIT from 12 to 1,000,000 and all 69 UI tests still passed. Add a fixture with more than 12 forward or reverse references that proves 12 are initially rendered, the exact remainder is disclosed, and explicit expansion reveals the rest.\n\nBuild one index in App and pass it into Thread; use it for the remaining record/decision lookups; build reverse buckets with push; and pin the reference bound. Then regenerate the embed, publish fresh exact-head artifacts for both ui and internal/service, and request a fresh review.\n\nArchitecture: the head changes layer 7 UI and internal/service's embedded distribution. It preserves the kernel, Workroom fold, projection, service-route, and two-screen contracts; no architecture-page update is required. The unresolved duplicate/quadratic indexing is an implementation failure within the surface, not an architecture contract change.\n\nSecurity: React renders projection values as text, no URL/HTML/credential/signature/mutation path is added, and unrooted records fail visibly as Gone rather than being granted request semantics. Reference output is intended to bound untrusted projection fan-out, but the absence of a load-bearing bound test leaves this availability control unresolved; that is part of the requested changes.\n\nSimplification: one projection index shared by App and Thread, mutable reverse-adjacency buckets during construction, and index reads in ElidedRecord/actionsFor are both simpler and asymptotically safer than the current duplicate structures and repeated scans.\n\nIndependent exact-head gates pass: git diff --check; make ui-check with a clean generated embed; npm test 69 pass, 0 fail; npx tsc --noEmit; go test ./internal/service/... ./internal/wireparity/.... The two temporary mutation worktrees were removed with git worktree remove. This verdict is for bd81fbb1 only. It does not judge or supersede the parallel ac2dd80f ratification-queue head, and neither exact-head verdict can bless a later combined conflict resolution.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f3e11bc8213b6a6b7ff111a1aa2e36f3b0fe982c","head":"bd81fbb15251ff949a8ebb984e70a8a933bcc729","verdict":"changes-requested"}}